On Fri, 25 Jul 2003 15:02:57 +0000, Jeremiah DeWitt Weiner wrote:

> 	Personally, I wouldn't mind seeing the magic system made a
> little more complex - maybe require certain objects or conditions for
> certain powerful spells, like "you must have a garnet and a bat corpse
> to cast spell X", or "you must be wielding a dagger and be next to a
> water square to cast spell Y".  Not for everything, of course, that
> would make spellcasting far too difficult.

I like this idea.  For some time, I have thought that the addition of some
component-based spells would be a great idea.  Hrm.. like what?

Well, how about this, for example:
 ===
#sorcery

Use what?

a + a diamond
b + a potion of gain energy
c + a blessed  + 1 elven dagger

[paralyzed for d20 turns]

Your elven dagger glows blue for a moment.

c - a blessed +2 elven dagger

===
Successful #sorcery consumes power (#sorcery could be treated as eighth
level spellcasting, requring 40 energy.) and destroys all involved
components except those at the purpose of the spell (the dagger in the
example above). Failed #sorcery consumes normal failed-spell power, and
destroys ALL of the components.  Both take time to perform.

In fact, how about gems as necessary components for all #sorcery?  It
would give them a useful purpose for after you've got the luckstone and
have wiped out the shops.   Or, a bit simpler, how about adding this
"sorcery" simply as a collection of level 8 (or higher) spellbooks and
spells, whose power is can be balanced by the necessity of finding proper
components and the destruction of said components?

I know this would be quite non-trivial to code, and I wish I had the
ability to do so, but I thought coming up with a few ideas might be fun,
at least.

Any more level 8 spell suggestions?

